<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edward Braddock</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1695">1695</a>–<a href="/years/1755">1755</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Edward Braddock was a British general sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> in 1755 as commander-in-chief at the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-and-indian-war">French and Indian War</a>. Leading an expedition against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-duquesne">Fort Duquesne</a>, he was defeated and mortally wounded by French and Native forces at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-monongahela">Battle of the Monongahela</a> on 9 July 1755. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a>, one of his aides, helped organise the retreat.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edward-braddock.jpg" alt="Edward Braddock" width="320" height="442" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: William Sartain · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edward_Braddock_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Braddock was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in January 1695, the son of Major-General Edward Braddock of the 2nd Regiment of Foot Guards. He was commissioned ensign in his father's regiment on 11 October 1710, at the age of 15, and became lieutenant of its grenadier company in 1716. On 26 May 1718 he fought a duel in Hyde Park with a colonel named Waller. Promotion was slow: he became captain only in 1736, at 41, major in 1743 and lieutenant colonel on 21 November 1745.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-austrian-succession">War of the Austrian Succession</a> he took part in the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bergen-op-zoom">Bergen op Zoom</a> in 1747. He was made colonel of the 14th Regiment of Foot on 17 February 1753 and major general the next year. He landed at Hampton, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>, with two infantry regiments on 20 February 1755, and at the Congress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> on 14 April colonial governors persuaded him to attack the French on four fronts: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-niagara">Fort Niagara</a>, Fort Saint-Frédéric at Crown Point under William Johnson, Fort Beausejour under Colonel Robert Monckton, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-duquesne">Fort Duquesne</a> under Braddock himself.</p><p>Delayed by confusion and by supplies the colonies had promised and not delivered, he had to cut a road for his artillery. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benjamin-franklin">Benjamin Franklin</a>'s autobiography describes helping him gather wagons and warning him of ambush. When his column had crossed the Monongahela, it met a French and Indian force from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-duquesne">Fort Duquesne</a> that worked down its flanks and caught it in crossfire. The causes of the defeat are still debated; one likely factor was that he underestimated how quickly his enemies could react and how fast his own men's discipline could fail.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Shot through the chest, he was carried from the field by Washington and Colonel Nicholas Meriwether and died on 13 July. He left Washington his sash and two pistols, and the sash is kept at Mount Vernon. He was buried in the road near Great Meadows, with wagons driven over the grave to hide it. Remains believed to be his were found in 1804 and reburied, and the Coldstream Guards raised a monument in 1913. Braddock in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a> and several roads bear his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Edward Braddock” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Seven Years War, French and Native forces defeated General <b>Edward Braddock</b> near the Monongahela in 1755, and the Marquis de Montcalm took Fort Oswego in 1756 and Fort William Henry in 1757 and won at Carillon in 1758.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-france">New France · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Perthshire, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=56.5,-4&amp;year=1734" aria-label="Perthshire on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Edward Braddock"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4811 5293h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4811 5293h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=56.5,-4&amp;year=1734">What was here in 1734? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
